art by adding the initial condition data $y(0)=1$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n$y^{′}|= 3x - 2y + 5; = 3 \\cdot 0 - 2 \\cdot 1 + 5; = 3$.\nSo, we add this to our table.$\\,x\\,$$\\,y\\,$$\\,y'\\,$$\\,y'_1\\,$$\\,y'_2\\,$$\\,y'_3\\,$$\\Delta y$$0$$1$$3$\n- The first intermediate point is\n$(x_{1}, y_{1})|= (x + \\frac{1}{2}Δx, y + \\frac{1}{2}y^{′} \\cdot Δx); = (0 + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1, 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1); = (\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{5}{2})$.\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n$y_{1}^{′}|= 3x_{1} - 2y_{1} + 5; = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} + 5; = \\frac{3}{2}$.\nWe can add this to our table.$\\,x\\,$$\\,y\\,$$\\,y'\\,$$\\,y'_1\\,$$\\,y'_2\\,$$\\,y'_3\\,$$\\Delta y$$0$$1$$3$$\\dfrac32$\n- The second intermediate point is\n$(x_{2}, y_{2})|= (x + \\frac{1}{2}Δx, y + \\frac{1}{2}y_{1}^{′} \\cdot Δx); = (0 + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1, 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 1); = (\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{7}{4})$.\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n$y_{2}^{′}|= 3x_{2} - 2y_{2} + 5; = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{4} + 5; = 3$.\nWe add this to our table.$\\,x\\,$$\\,y\\,$$\\,y'\\,$$\\,y'_1\\,$$\\,y'_2\\,$$\\,y'_3\\,$$\\Delta y$$0$$1$$3$$\\dfrac32$$3$\n- The third intermediate point is\n$(x_{3}, y_{3})|= (x + Δx, y + y_{2}^{′} \\cdot Δx); = (0 + 1, 1 + 3 \\cdot 1); = (1, 4)$.\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n$y_{3}^{′}|= 3x_{3} - 2y_{3} + 5; = 3 \\cdot 1 - 2 \\cdot 4 + 5; = 0$.\nWe can add this to our table.$\\,x\\,$$\\,y\\,$$\\,y'\\,$$\\,y'_1\\,$$\\,y'_2\\,$$\\,y'_3\\,$$\\Delta y$$0$$1$$3$$\\dfrac32$$3$$0$\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= \\frac{1}{6}(y^{′} + 2y_{1}^{′} + 2y_{2}^{′} + y_{3}^{′}) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(3 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} + 2 \\cdot 3 + 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx \\\\\n&= 0 + 1 \\\\\n&= 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 1 + 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nLooking at the two leftmost columns, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1) \\approx 3\n$$" 13194139533438 17592186045585 false] [17592186167995 1064 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x - 2y + 5, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nOur goal is to approximate $y(1)$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\nSince we know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1),$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nLet's use a table to keep track of all important values, including the intermediate slopes. We start by adding the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =3x-2y+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{'} & =3{x}_{1}-2{y}_{1}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{'} & =3{x}_{2}-2{y}_{2}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{'} & =3{x}_{3}-2{y}_{3}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 4+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{'}+2{y}_{1}^{'}+2{y}_{2}^{'}+{y}_{3}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 3+0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nLooking at the two leftmost columns, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 3.$" 13194139533438 17592186045585 true] [17592186168039 1064 "Let's go back to our previous initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x-2y+5, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nCarrying out the Runge-Kutta method resulted in the following table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nWe can view each step of our approximation in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n- The first intermediate point $(x_1, y_1) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac52\\right)$ is found using the slope of the initial point $y'=3.$\n\n![](images/ca/cabf5d9d55f4840f2df6d90fbeb304c5235f98cd67647903ebd1f59c6a3819cc.png)\n\n- The second intermediate point $(x_2, y_2) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac74\\right)$ is found using the slope of the previous intermediate point $y'_1=\\dfrac32.$\n\n![](images/21/213e765c0f866b24028bdddbe1f3c709bdfec83b5e84bbe9ec9b97906f50970a.png)\n\n- The third intermediate point $(x_3, y_3) = (1,4)$ is found using the slope of the previous intermediate point $y'_2=3.$\n\n![](images/76/769e51f64f8ba9a0c2c0bc8ae648e33e33ff4d4ea5bc6fbd73ddfdfad1d05481.png)\n\n- Finally, we compute the increment in $y$ by taking a weighted average of the four sample slopes.\n\n![](images/c6/c6b5f34e6cc55733c2dfdc2faeb0333c9b214d54a88abc0baea801dae3764a3f.png)" 13194139533438 17592186045585 true] [17592186168039 1064 "Let's go back to our previous initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x-2y+5, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nCarrying out the Runge-Kutta method resulted in the following table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\,y'_1\\,$ | $\\,y'_2\\,$ | $\\,y'_3\\,$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ | $3$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nWe can view each step of our approximation in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n- The first intermediate point $(x_1, y_1) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac52\\right)$ is found using the slope of the initial point $y'=3$.![](images/ca/cabf5d9d55f4840f2df6d90fbeb304c5235f98cd67647903ebd1f59c6a3819cc.png)\n\n- The second intermediate point $(x_2, y_2) = \\left(\\dfrac12, \\dfrac74\\right)$ is found using the slope of the previous intermediate point $y'_1=\\dfrac32$.![](images/21/213e765c0f866b24028bdddbe1f3c709bdfec83b5e84bbe9ec9b97906f50970a.png)\n- The third intermediate point $(x_3, y_3) = (1,4)$ is found using the slope of the previous intermediate point $y'_2=3$.![](images/76/769e51f64f8ba9a0c2c0bc8ae648e33e33ff4d4ea5bc6fbd73ddfdfad1d05481.png)\n- Finally, we compute the increment in $y$ by taking a weighted average of the four sample slopes.![](images/c6/c6b5f34e6cc55733c2dfdc2faeb0333c9b214d54a88abc0baea801dae3764a3f.png)" 13194139533438 17592186045585 false] [17592186168046 1064 "When an integrand contains a product of trigonometric functions, we can often rewrite that product as a sum using the sum and difference formulas. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (α - β) &= \\cos α\\cos β + \\sin α\\sin β \\\\\n\\cos (α + β) &= \\cos α\\cos β - \\sin α\\sin β\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta$, we obtain the following **product-to-sum identity**:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\cos(5x)\\cos(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$.\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (5x)\\cos (3x) &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (5x - 3x) + \\cos (5x + 3x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (2x) + \\cos (8x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\cos (5x)\\cos (3x)dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2x) + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (8x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\sin (2x) + \\frac{1}{16}\\sin (8x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533438 17592186045585 false] [17592186168046 1064 "When an integrand contains a product of trigonometric functions, we can often rewrite that product as a sum using the sum and difference formulas. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following **product-to-sum identity**:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\cos(5x)\\cos(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(5x)\\operatorname{cos}(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}(5x)\\operatorname{cos}(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}(8x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533438 17592186045585 true] [17592186168047 1064 "Similar to the above, when an integrand contains a product of sines, we can rewrite that product as a sum of cosines using the sum and difference formulas for cosine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (α - β) &= \\cos α\\cos β + \\sin α\\sin β \\\\\n\\cos (α + β) &= \\cos α\\cos β - \\sin α\\sin β\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) - \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta$, we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)-\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(7x)\\sin(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$.\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (7x)\\sin (4x) &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (7x - 4x) - \\cos (7x + 4x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (3x) - \\cos (11x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sin (7x)\\sin (4x)dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (3x) - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (11x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\sin (3x) - \\frac{1}{22}\\sin (11x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533438 17592186045585 false] [17592186168047 1064 "Similar to the above, when an integrand contains a product of sines, we can rewrite that product as a sum of cosines using the sum and difference formulas for cosine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) - \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)-\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(7x)\\sin(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(7x)\\operatorname{sin}(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(7x-4x)-\\operatorname{cos}(7x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}(7x)\\operatorname{sin}(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}(3x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}(11x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533438 17592186045585 true] [17592186168048 1064 "Next, when an integrand contains a product of sine and cosine, we can rewrite that product as a sum of sines using the sum and difference formulas for sine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (α + β) &= \\sin α\\cos β + \\cos α\\sin β \\\\\n\\sin (α - β) &= \\sin α\\cos β - \\cos α\\sin β\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta) + \\sin(\\alpha-\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta$, we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(8x)\\cos(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$.\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (8x)\\cos (2x) &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin (8x + 2x) + \\sin (8x - 2x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin (10x) + \\sin (6x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sin (8x)\\cos (2x)dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (10x) + \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (6x)dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{20}\\cos (10x) - \\frac{1}{12}\\cos (6x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533438 17592186045585 false] [17592186168048 1064 "Next, when an integrand contains a product of sine and cosine, we can rewrite that product as a sum of sines using the sum and difference formulas for sine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta) + \\sin(\\alpha-\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(8x)\\cos(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(8x)\\operatorname{cos}(2x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}(8x+2x)+\\operatorname{sin}(8x-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}(8x)\\operatorname{cos}(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}(10x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}(6x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533438 17592186045585 true] [17592186168080 1064 "Let's consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = 2y(1-y)\n$$\n\nThe slope field for this equation is shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b53d7d2a486719ea787ce84f5d2b18de656dd1013625914af740e2f4db7e1319.png)\n\nLet's see how much information we can gather about the differential equation by referring *only* to its slope field.\n\n- First, note that the slope field has horizontal symmetry. Therefore, the associated differential equation must be of the form $y' = f(y)$ (i.e., it must be autonomous). Indeed, the equation $y' = 