g) to a vector ![](images/ec/ec2c632c5fe5848c1f26ac23f4d271bfa5295e86f9b7bd512d215d5ff3844cba.svg) in ![](images/b2/b2854737dd2d2e96422a242158c3b44004d7f327822db17f49758cf92ad880db.svg)" 13194139533438 17592186045585 true] [17592186173205 1064 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2$.\nA **vector field** on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a *vector* $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/f7/f7208c8cae2a40066b223ae4e7612500b0f553c4cee5368e79bcd05dea87272b.png)\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = (x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (x-y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nThis vector field maps the point $(2,1)$ to the vector $3\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(2, 1) &= (2 + 1)i + (2 - 1)j = 3i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, any vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ can be expressed in terms of its component functions $P$ and $Q$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nIn the example above, we had\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = x+y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x-y\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can easily extend our definition of a vector field to three-dimensional or even $n$-dimensional space. For example, if $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$, then a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3$." 13194139533438 17592186045585 false] [17592186173206 1064 "We can find the domain of a vector field by calculating the intersection of the domains of its component functions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\ln\\left(4-x^2-y^2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwhose component functions are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\ln\\left(4-x^2-y^2\\right), \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{x-1}\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf F$, we find the domain of each component function and then compute their intersection.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n$4-x^2-y^2 \\gt 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2 \\lt 4$\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n$D_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: x^2+y^2 \\lt 4 \\big\\}$,\nwhich is the interior of a circle of radius $2$, as shown below:\n\n![](images/0b/0b76751de4305ed2cd3410ea52602756dc05c6d4f5c2a848e2c52220e3a445d1.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n$x-1 \\geq 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x \\geq 1$\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n$D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: x \\geq 1 \\big\\}$,\nwhich is the half-plane shown below:\n\n![](images/88/88dee5adf4ac85267164d42c2256c8ae25dae0ac9e74284e04186ef54c057811.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = D_1 \\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: x^2 + y^2 \\lt 4, \\, x\\geq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $\\mathbf F$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c7/c7ec35df55bcb910098888ff32cef5566c522c5d469e94409cd96e8762a63a8f.png)" 13194139533438 17592186045585 false] [17592186173206 1064 "We can find the domain of a vector field by calculating the intersection of the domains of its component functions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\ln\\left(4-x^2-y^2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhose component functions are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\ln\\left(4-x^2-y^2\\right), \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we find the domain of each component function and then compute their intersection.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the 